compared to Euclidean methods at that time. ApoVAE [118] also deals with the dialog-response generation problem. It optimized the variational bound by adversarially training and exploited the primal-dual formulation of KL divergence based on the Fenchel duality [127]. In the task of word embeddings, work [108] proposed a Gaussian-like distribution in hyperbolic space, which is called pseudo-hyperbolic Gaussian. Based on this a hyperbolic VAE is presented to deal with the word embeddings. Work [74] adapted the Glove [75] algorithm to learn unsupervised word embeddings in this type of Riemannian manifolds. To this end, they proposed to embed words in a Cartesian product of hyperbolic spaces which they theoretically connected to the Gaussian word embeddings and their Fisher geometry. We can see some notable founds in the experiments of Hypernymy [161] evaluation task. Based on their method, the fully unsupervised model can almost outperform all supervised Euclidean counterparts. Once trained with a small amount of weakly supervision for the hypernymy score, they can obtain significant improvements and this result is much better that the models in Euclidean space. In the taxonomy embedding task, work [25] is one pioneer research piece that can learn embeddings in hyperbolic space (Poincar ́e model). They performed experiments on the tasks of embedding of taxonomies. To evaluate the ability of the embeddings to infer hierarchical relationships without supervision, they trained on data where the hierarchy of objects is not explicitly encoded. A significant improvement was witnessed in the Taxonomy Embedding task. Work [66] proposed a re-parametrization of Poincar ́e embeddings that removes the need for the projection step and allows the use of any of the popular optimization techniques in deep learning, such as Adam. Work [141] pointed out that the Lorentz model is substantially more efficient than in the Poincar ́e model. Therefore, they proposed to learn a continuous representation using Lorentz model, which further improves the performance in this task. Work [92] mentioned that Poincar ́e distance is numerically unstable and explained why the squared Lorentzian distance is a better choice than the Poincare ́ metric. Based on this, they learnt a closed-form squared Lorentzian distance and thus further improve the performance on the Taxonomy Embedding task. Very recently, work [15] shows the superior parameter efficiency of their methods compared to conventional hyperbolic components, and the stability and outperform over their Euclidean counterparts. They evaluated their Poincar ́e MLR on the WordNet with the one proposed in [23], as well as the counterpart in Euclidean space. As mentioned before, work [76] constructs the hyperbolic model for dealing with the numerical instability of the previous hyperbolic networks. Based on the Lorentz model, they provide a very efficient model to learn the embedding. For instance, they can even compress the embeddings down to 2% of a Poincar ́e embedding of the onWordNet Nouns. In the task of taxonomy induction, which is aiming at creating a semantic hierarchy of entities by using hyponym-hypernym relations, traditional approaches like extraction of lexicalsyntactic patterns [162] and co-occurrence information [163] are still dominating in the state-of-the-art taxonomy learning. Currently, work [144] proposed to employ Poincar ́e embeddings in this task. Specifically, they first created a domain-specific Poincar ́e embedding. Based on this, they identified and relocated the outliers, as well as attached the unconnected terms. Textual entailment, which is also called natural language inference, is a binary classification task to predict whether the
